1
00:00:00,167 --> 00:00:03,667
When a retired janitor
in Vermont died in 2014,

2
00:00:03,667 --> 00:00:07,333
his will revealed he was
worth almost $8 million.

3
00:00:07,367 --> 00:00:09,500
Ronald Read grew up poor on a Vermont farm

4
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:12,267
and was the first in his
family to finish high school.

5
00:00:12,467 --> 00:00:15,733
After the war, he pumped
gas for about 25 years,

6
00:00:15,733 --> 00:00:19,733
then worked as a part-time
janitor at JCPenney for 17 more.

7
00:00:19,933 --> 00:00:24,500
He drove a second-hand Toyota Yaris, held
his coat together with a safety pin,

8
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:27,233
and chopped his own
firewood into his nineties.

9
00:00:27,267 --> 00:00:30,533
A stranger once paid for his meal,
thinking he couldn't afford it.

10
00:00:30,667 --> 00:00:32,433
What nobody knew: for decades,

11
00:00:32,433 --> 00:00:36,867
he'd quietly bought shares in companies
he understood, and held on to them.

12
00:00:37,067 --> 00:00:40,367
When he died, he owned at least 95 stocks.

13
00:00:40,567 --> 00:00:44,367
The certificates in his safe deposit
box stood about 5 inches high.

14
00:00:44,600 --> 00:00:45,867
Not every pick worked.

15
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:48,800
He held Lehman Brothers
when it collapsed in 2008.

16
00:00:48,967 --> 00:00:50,900
It was one stock out of dozens.

17
00:00:51,133 --> 00:00:57,867
Then came the will: $4.8 million to the
local hospital, $1.2 million to the library.

18
00:00:57,900 --> 00:00:59,667
Twice its yearly budget.

19
00:00:59,667 --> 00:01:02,400
In 2023, the hospital opened a new wing,

20
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:06,100
partly paid for by his gift:
the Ronald Read Pavilion.

21
00:01:06,267 --> 00:01:07,800
He never earned a big salary.

22
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:09,767
He just gave his money time.

